WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:02.244
- [Man] Yes, hello.

00:00:02.244 --> 00:00:03.077
- [Gary] Hello.

00:00:03.077 --> 00:00:03.910
- [Man] Great to meet you.

00:00:03.910 --> 00:00:04.743
- [Gary] Nice to meet you.

00:00:04.743 --> 00:00:05.576
How are you?

00:00:05.576 --> 00:00:06.409
- Good, very good.
- Good, good.

00:00:06.409 --> 00:00:07.242
- Hello, everyone.

00:00:07.242 --> 00:00:08.075
- A few words.

00:00:08.075 --> 00:00:10.730
This is our participants
of the FLY program,

00:00:10.730 --> 00:00:11.960
something we started new

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:14.050
basically teaching or showing

00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:16.000
bigger companies how you can think and act

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.420
more of like start-ups,

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:20.100
to be more in love with it.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:23.976
So, it was something you
can certainly talk about.

00:00:23.976 --> 00:00:24.809
- Sure.

00:00:24.809 --> 00:00:25.751
(laughing)

00:00:25.751 --> 00:00:26.752
- So, we were thinking if you could talk

00:00:26.752 --> 00:00:28.220
maybe a little bit about entrepreneurship

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:29.600
what could be motivating.

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:32.300
- Sure, um, hello.
- Hey.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:35.250
So, I think what's super interesting to me

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:38.257
is the last eight or nine years of my life

00:00:38.257 --> 00:00:40.620
I've been spending most of my time

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:42.210
with Fortune 100 companies

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:43.070
with my agency.

00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:47.760
I think, without side-railing
whatever you're preaching,

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:51.640
I think the biggest issue that we,

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:53.540
who have a point of view from the outside

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:54.400
of bigger companies,

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:59.060
have is sometimes too much
of an ideology, you know?

00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:02.330
I've become unbelievably
empathetic to a lot of you

00:01:02.330 --> 00:01:05.750
in what I didn't know coming
from a family business

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:07.871
and coming from start up culture

00:01:07.871 --> 00:01:12.768
is most of the companies
that are big in the world

00:01:12.768 --> 00:01:15.553
care about their finances every 90 days.

00:01:16.730 --> 00:01:19.274
And, so, he can inspire you

00:01:19.274 --> 00:01:22.390
for the next fucking
six years of your life.

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:23.370
You can, you know...

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:26.150
There's nuances that you can pick up on,

00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:28.090
and there's probably little tactics

00:01:28.090 --> 00:01:30.597
that could create little moments

00:01:30.597 --> 00:01:31.864
within your organization,

00:01:31.864 --> 00:01:34.460
but I'm no longer delusional about this,

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:38.660
and I have no interest in
pandering to an ideology.

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:40.400
I think all of you know the issue at hand

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:42.750
which is your organization's
lack of appetite

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:44.600
for the investment that's required

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:45.810
to make meaningful change

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:49.683
during a major macro
consumer and economic shift

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:54.030
of the growth of the internet.

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:55.920
I mean, this is...

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:57.380
It's going to be very hard for all of us

00:01:57.380 --> 00:01:59.270
to muster up enough energy

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:01.400
and ability to move these things.

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:02.331
And, not to make it

00:02:02.331 --> 00:02:04.310
there's nothing we can do,

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:07.320
but I think the reality is...

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:08.460
I would say this.

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:10.760
The most meaningful conversations I've had

00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:12.600
with the biggest of the biggest companies

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:16.690
is instead of me pontificating
that you should do this,

00:02:16.690 --> 00:02:17.940
why don't we have a conversation

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:20.578
of what's gonna happen in
the next three to 11 years

00:02:20.578 --> 00:02:22.190
whenever it's your time

00:02:22.190 --> 00:02:24.300
to actually really get hurt by this

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:26.700
when you'll be willing
to do something about it.

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:29.500
Your companies aren't gonna do shit

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:30.853
until they have to.

00:02:32.510 --> 00:02:34.220
Right? So...

00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:36.330
I think the biggest thing you can do

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:38.113
is get them ready for the marathon.

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:41.400
Right, so I think about running.

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:42.913
If all these big companies,

00:02:42.913 --> 00:02:44.310
I have no sense of the
companies in this room,

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:45.610
but if big companies

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:48.900
are gonna be sucked into

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:51.451
adjusting to the reality of a new world,

00:02:51.451 --> 00:02:53.297
I think everybody here understands

00:02:53.297 --> 00:02:56.672
that there's very few big
companies in the world

00:02:56.672 --> 00:03:00.170
that don't have most of
their money and energy

00:03:00.170 --> 00:03:01.810
tied up into an infrastructure

00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:03.360
that is becoming less relevant.

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:06.310
And, so, that's why it's gonna happen,

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:09.065
and so how do you get
them on the treadmill?

00:03:09.065 --> 00:03:12.700
Like, for the sake of
what you're doing here,

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:14.860
you've gotta get your
company on a treadmill

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:16.210
or eating a little bit better.

00:03:16.210 --> 00:03:17.930
Not running the marathon yet, right?

00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:20.050
What can you do to make incremental steps

00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:22.583
so that when an Uber or Air BNB

00:03:22.583 --> 00:03:25.741
or real Netflix or a big event happens

00:03:25.741 --> 00:03:27.442
in whatever industry you're in.

00:03:27.442 --> 00:03:28.850
And, that's what's happening, right?

00:03:28.850 --> 00:03:30.647
If you went around and said,

00:03:30.647 --> 00:03:31.480
"I'm in this,

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:32.313
"I'm in this,"

00:03:32.313 --> 00:03:33.198
in my head I'm like,

00:03:33.198 --> 00:03:34.197
"Okay, in the next 24 months,

00:03:34.197 --> 00:03:35.207
"in the next 72 months,

00:03:35.207 --> 00:03:36.224
"in the next 96 months."

00:03:36.224 --> 00:03:38.230
Eventually, all of our numbers

00:03:38.230 --> 00:03:39.130
are gonna come up.

00:03:40.769 --> 00:03:44.580
Network television
across the globe is dead

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:46.430
because now we have OTT,

00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:47.350
and whether it's Netflix,

00:03:47.350 --> 00:03:48.183
or something in Asia,

00:03:48.183 --> 00:03:49.600
or something, you know, or Amazon,

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:51.210
it's over.

00:03:51.210 --> 00:03:53.410
Having a satellite in
the air is a bad idea.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:56.930
Right? Having your retailers.

00:03:56.930 --> 00:03:59.610
It's not that eCommerce is
gonna take all the business.

00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:01.140
It's just that there's enough being done

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:04.083
on commerce that the leases
aren't a good deal anymore.

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:06.040
So, it doesn't take a...

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:06.873
I think the other thing

00:04:06.873 --> 00:04:08.690
to think about as a theme

00:04:08.690 --> 00:04:09.580
is it doesn't take...

00:04:09.580 --> 00:04:11.600
It's not like whatever you're doing

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:13.920
gets replaced by some other new thing.

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:16.250
It's it doesn't take a lot of market share

00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:17.790
taken away from you

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:19.240
for the whole thing to break.

00:04:20.510 --> 00:04:22.550
So, those are the themes
that I see going on here.

00:04:22.550 --> 00:04:24.693
So, I think, you know,

00:04:24.693 --> 00:04:26.430
for you and you,

00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:29.060
and the key is not for
us to be ideological

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:30.080
and say, "They get it."

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:31.420
You know, we think they don't get it.

00:04:31.420 --> 00:04:32.820
They get it.

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:34.970
They're just playing by different rules,

00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:37.010
and they can't get it done.

00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:39.010
So, I think instead of
spending all this time

00:04:39.010 --> 00:04:40.930
on ideology that is great

00:04:40.930 --> 00:04:42.920
for one day of inspiration,

00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:45.870
but then next Thursday you
can't do anything about it,

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:46.930
I'd challenge you guys

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:48.410
to think about curriculum

00:04:48.410 --> 00:04:50.748
that actually makes something practical

00:04:50.748 --> 00:04:52.620
that can move them...

00:04:54.190 --> 00:04:55.023
Right?

00:04:57.160 --> 00:04:58.110
'Cause it's coming.

00:04:59.480 --> 00:05:00.797
For real.

00:05:00.797 --> 00:05:01.630
(mumbling from audience)

00:05:01.630 --> 00:05:02.850
Yeah, you know, for me,

00:05:02.850 --> 00:05:04.150
'cause I've been in it for a long time,

00:05:04.150 --> 00:05:06.519
it was question the decade ago.

00:05:06.519 --> 00:05:07.960
Is it a fad?

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:08.986
Is it small?

00:05:08.986 --> 00:05:12.687
It hasn't even... (sighs)

00:05:12.687 --> 00:05:15.290
The internet is...

00:05:15.290 --> 00:05:17.063
This is the secondary life.

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:20.014
This is the real life.

00:05:20.014 --> 00:05:21.037
It just is.

00:05:21.037 --> 00:05:22.419
- [Audience] That's scary.

00:05:22.419 --> 00:05:23.252
(audience mumbles)

00:05:23.252 --> 00:05:24.427
- And, Tara, to that point,

00:05:24.427 --> 00:05:27.410
a lot of people are
making business decisions

00:05:27.410 --> 00:05:29.787
based on how they feel about it,

00:05:29.787 --> 00:05:33.760
and that's dangerous, you know?

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:35.780
It's very dangerous to
make business decisions

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:38.473
based on your personal
point of view of technology.

00:05:40.590 --> 00:05:41.890
And, I don't know if you guys

00:05:41.890 --> 00:05:42.830
got to see my speech or not,

00:05:42.830 --> 00:05:44.350
but that's what I was trying
to talk about up there,

00:05:44.350 --> 00:05:46.574
which is be practical.

00:05:46.574 --> 00:05:48.954
Like, know what you're
actually talking about.

00:05:48.954 --> 00:05:50.870
I don't know what you're talking,

00:05:50.870 --> 00:05:51.820
probably organizational,

00:05:51.820 --> 00:05:53.095
but that's why I spend time on marketing

00:05:53.095 --> 00:05:57.044
because marketing you can
actually change the money.

00:05:57.044 --> 00:05:59.320
You can actually move a million dollars

00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:01.160
from Google AdWords to Facebook,

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:04.920
or from bus stop ads to Instagram,

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:06.430
where as a lot of the heady shit,

00:06:06.430 --> 00:06:09.730
it's so inherently deep
in the organization.

00:06:09.730 --> 00:06:10.820
You can't move it

00:06:11.690 --> 00:06:13.940
'cause there's a human
that's gonna stop you.

00:06:15.010 --> 00:06:17.640
So, that's what I would...

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:19.863
As you think about trying to bring value,

00:06:20.990 --> 00:06:23.660
it's more practicality

00:06:23.660 --> 00:06:25.510
because I think there's
a little bit of audacity

00:06:25.510 --> 00:06:27.160
amongst us in the start up world.

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:29.073
They know.

00:06:30.760 --> 00:06:32.603
They just can't do anything about it.

00:06:33.778 --> 00:06:35.430
- [Man] That's something we talk

00:06:35.430 --> 00:06:36.580
quite a lot about actually.

00:06:36.580 --> 00:06:38.390
Can we do something about it,

00:06:38.390 --> 00:06:40.073
and what can we do?

00:06:40.073 --> 00:06:41.630
I think what to demonstrate

00:06:41.630 --> 00:06:43.500
is the small steps.

00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:45.803
Like, something I hope they
are getting involved with.

00:06:45.803 --> 00:06:48.763
- I think HR is a very interesting place,

00:06:49.792 --> 00:06:52.280
as I've learned more about big business.

00:06:52.280 --> 00:06:54.780
HR's a place where companies
waste a lot of money.

00:06:56.441 --> 00:06:57.450
You know, nobody kind of questions

00:06:57.450 --> 00:06:59.050
the HR budget for employees.

00:06:59.050 --> 00:07:00.000
It looks good,

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:02.010
but then the money's being spent on things

00:07:02.010 --> 00:07:03.860
that are just checking the box,

00:07:03.860 --> 00:07:06.322
so one of the places I've seen
fruitful for organizations,

00:07:06.322 --> 00:07:07.800
if you're lucky enough to have

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:10.650
an open-minded or flexible HR lead,

00:07:10.650 --> 00:07:11.565
is she or he,

00:07:11.565 --> 00:07:13.100
you can team up with them,

00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:13.933
or maybe you're in it,

00:07:13.933 --> 00:07:15.070
or maybe you have part...

00:07:15.070 --> 00:07:16.400
Again, I don't have context for the room,

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:18.670
but that's often times a place

00:07:18.670 --> 00:07:20.020
where I've seen some
really interesting things

00:07:20.020 --> 00:07:22.320
'cause then you can go into education.

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:23.370
And, ultimately,

00:07:23.370 --> 00:07:24.203
I don't love the way

00:07:24.203 --> 00:07:26.130
school is done in the world,

00:07:26.130 --> 00:07:28.160
but education's the game.

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:29.392
And, the funny part is,

00:07:29.392 --> 00:07:31.070
I made a comment up there.

00:07:31.070 --> 00:07:32.250
A lot of you have opinions

00:07:32.250 --> 00:07:33.870
about Facebook ads and Instagram ads,

00:07:33.870 --> 00:07:35.620
but you've never run one.

00:07:35.620 --> 00:07:37.160
The biggest thing that I...

00:07:37.160 --> 00:07:38.530
The reason that I think
I have a lot of success

00:07:38.530 --> 00:07:40.400
is I'm a business man

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:42.240
and then I care about all this stuff.

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:44.052
So, when I get to the C-Suite

00:07:44.052 --> 00:07:47.360
or the board of big companies,

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:48.613
I talk about business.

00:07:49.620 --> 00:07:52.510
And, when they get even a
little educated about business,

00:07:52.510 --> 00:07:54.240
not, "It's cool,"

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:56.067
not, "We should do it."

00:07:57.340 --> 00:07:59.380
When it's about the P&amp;L,

00:07:59.380 --> 00:08:01.000
when it's about money,

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:02.410
they start listening a lot more.

00:08:02.410 --> 00:08:03.639
- [Woman] You can bet on it.

00:08:03.639 --> 00:08:04.472
- 100 percent.

00:08:04.472 --> 00:08:05.440
To me, every time I talk about HR,

00:08:05.440 --> 00:08:07.242
every time I talk about innovation,

00:08:07.242 --> 00:08:10.053
every time I talk to the CFO,

00:08:11.410 --> 00:08:12.500
the reason I believe in people

00:08:12.500 --> 00:08:14.650
is turnover is expensive

00:08:14.650 --> 00:08:16.070
so take care of your people.

00:08:16.070 --> 00:08:16.903
It's money.

00:08:17.860 --> 00:08:19.160
The reason I believe in innovation

00:08:19.160 --> 00:08:21.460
is you're wasting so much
money as a big company

00:08:21.460 --> 00:08:22.520
on all these other things.

00:08:22.520 --> 00:08:24.070
Are you not willing to put a little money

00:08:24.070 --> 00:08:25.220
into where we're going?

00:08:26.120 --> 00:08:27.761
So, I think that's the other thing is

00:08:27.761 --> 00:08:30.710
make none of this ideological.

00:08:30.710 --> 00:08:32.710
Make all of it financial.

00:08:32.710 --> 00:08:34.386
'Cause that's what companies respond to.

00:08:34.386 --> 00:08:37.469
(mellow organ music)

